# poweredge-xubuntu.expected-plan — every mutating command
# `../storage-layout.sh --dry-run profiles/poweredge-xubuntu.env` plans against
# a BARE node, in order, byte for byte.
#
# WHY IT IS COMMITTED. This node's plan is the one the whole-device path has
# always produced; a change to the script that alters it is either a deliberate
# re-derivation of this node's rebuild or an accident. `--dry-run` prints the
# plan and nothing else asserts the WHOLE of it: `storage-layout-exit-tests.sh`
# §B asserts an ORDERED SUBSET, so a step silently added, dropped or reworded
# between two asserted rungs passes §B unnoticed. §F2 compares the run against
# this file as an equality, so it does not.
#
# The lines below are the `+ `-prefixed lines of that dry run with the `+ `
# stripped, captured against the suite's bare-node fake tools: no partition
# table, no filesystem signature, no volume group and no logical volume exists,
# and the controller CLI is absent (hence the unresolved `<enclosure>`
# placeholder that §B5 separately proves resolves to 32 on the real node).
#
# WHAT THE STAGE-1 TOOL PREFLIGHT ADDS HERE: NOTHING, EVER. Under
# `whole-device` the preflight never installs anything — this plan runs from the
# Recovery USB, which is built to carry the tools, so an absent one is a refusal
# rather than a package to fetch. A `--dry-run` prints that refusal as a
# `WOULD REFUSE: <tool> absent (<package>)` NOTE, which carries no `+ ` prefix
# and so is not part of this capture; `storage-layout-exit-tests.sh` §H4-H5 run
# this profile on a PATH without pvcreate or mkfs.xfs and assert both halves —
# the two WOULD REFUSE lines, and that the printed sequence is still byte-for-
# byte this file.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE IT. Only by deciding that this node's rebuild plan should
# change, and saying so in the change that alters it. Re-capturing this file to
# make a red §F2 go green is the same inversion `README.md` §"The profile is
# the record" forbids for the facts table: the failure is the finding.
/opt/MegaRAID/perccli/perccli64 /c0 add vd type=raid5 drives=<enclosure>:0-6 strip=64 wb ra direct
udevadm settle
sgdisk --zap-all /dev/sda
sgdisk --new=1:0:+1G --typecode=1:ef00 --change-name=1:ESP /dev/sda
sgdisk --new=2:0:0 --typecode=2:8e00 --change-name=2:lvm /dev/sda
partprobe /dev/sda
pvcreate --yes /dev/sda2
pvcreate --yes /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-WD_BLACK_SN8100_4000GB_25384T801085
pvcreate --yes /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-WD_BLACK_SN8100_4000GB_25374X802154
vgcreate poweredge /dev/sda2
vgcreate nvmea /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-WD_BLACK_SN8100_4000GB_25384T801085
vgcreate nvmeb /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-WD_BLACK_SN8100_4000GB_25374X802154
lvcreate --yes -L 200G -n root poweredge
lvcreate --yes -L 8G -n swap poweredge
lvcreate --yes -L 1T -n ci-cache poweredge
lvcreate --yes -L 1.5T -n ci-containerd nvmea
lvcreate --yes -L 1.5T -n ci-workvols nvmeb
mkfs.vfat -F 32 -n ESP /dev/sda1
mkfs.ext4 -q -L root /dev/poweredge/root
mkswap -L swap /dev/poweredge/swap
mkfs.ext4 -q -L ci-cache /dev/poweredge/ci-cache
mkfs.ext4 -q -L ci-containerd /dev/nvmea/ci-containerd
mkfs.xfs -q -m reflink=1 -L ci-workvols /dev/nvmeb/ci-workvols
